{"index":0,"content":"\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a C++20 function that:\n1. Validates balanced brackets (parentheses, square brackets, curly braces)\n2. Ignores brackets inside quoted strings (both single and double quotes)\n3. Includes a compact self-test\n4. Return code only\n\nLet me think about the design:\n\n- Iterate through the string character by character\n- Track whether we're inside a single-quoted or double-quoted string\n- Handle escape characters (backslash) inside strings\n- When not in a string, push opening brackets onto a stack, pop and match on closing brackets\n- At the end, the stack must be empty\n\nFor C++20, I can use `std::string_view`, `constexpr`, and modern features.\n\nLet me write this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n#include <string_view>\n#include <stack>\n#include <cassert>\n#include <cstdio>\n\nbool balanced(std::string_view s) {\n    std::stack<char> st;\n    char quote = 0;\n    bool escaped = false;\n    \n    for (char c : s) {\n        if (escaped) {\n            escaped = false;\n            continue;\n        }\n        if (c == '\\\\' && quote) {\n            escaped = true;\n            continue;\n        }\n        if (quote) {\n            if (c == quote) quote = 0;\n            continue;\n        }\n        if (c == '\"' || c == '\\'') {\n            quote = c;\n            continue;\n        }\n        if (c == '(' || c == '[' || c == '{') {\n            st.push(c);\n        } else if (c == ')' || c == ']' || c == '}') {\n            if (st.empty()) return false;\n            char top = st.top(); st.pop();\n            if ((c == ')' && top != '(') ||\n                (c == ']' && top != '[') ||\n                (c == '}' && top != '{'))\n                return false;\n        }\n    }\n    return st.empty() && quote == 0;\n}\n```\n\nWait, should I return false if we end inside an unterminated string? That seems reasonable.\n\nNow for the self-test, I'll make it compact with a few test cases:\n\n- `\"\"` -> true\n","toke